<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#stato-da-mar">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stato da Mar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Stato da Màr</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Stato da Mar ('State of the Sea') was the collective name for the overseas possessions of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, as distinct from its Italian mainland territories, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domini-di-terraferma">Domini di Terraferma</a>. Built up from the 11th century and especially after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> of 1204, it included Istria, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>, coastal Albania, the Ionian Islands, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and ports and islands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, protecting Venice's trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stato-da-mar.jpg" alt="Stato da Mar" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maximilian Dörrbecker (Chumwa) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Venezianische_Kolonien.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Stato da Màr, also called the Domini da Mar, was one of the three subdivisions of the possessions of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, beside the Dogado, Venice proper, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domini-di-terraferma">Domini di Terraferma</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Its creation began around the year 1000, when Doge Pietro II Orseolo defeated the Narentines and the Dalmatian city-states recognised Venetian rule, so that the doge styled himself Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> for some decades. Venetian control of Dalmatia was not stable until the early 15th century. In the 12th and 13th centuries Venice gradually established its rule over Istria, which it kept until the end of the Republic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The overseas domains reached their widest nominal extent after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> in 1204, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> declared that it had acquired three octaves of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. Most of this was never under Venetian control and was held by the Greek successor states, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-nicaea">Empire of Nicaea</a>. Venice held the post of Podestà in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> until the Byzantine reconquest of 1261, and of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> gains it kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a> until the 15th century, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyclades">Cyclades</a> until the 16th and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> until the 17th.</p><p>A new phase of growth followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-chioggia">War of Chioggia</a>. Corfu came under permanent Venetian rule in 1386, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durazzo</a> on the Albanian coast in 1392 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Scutari</a> in 1396. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ankara">Battle of Ankara</a> of 1402 and the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a> of 1403 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> gained a string of Greek fortresses, among them Lepanto in 1407 and Patras in 1408. In 1409 it bought several Dalmatian domains, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zadar">Zadar</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ladislaus-of-naples">Ladislaus of Naples</a> for 100,000 ducats, and in 1489 it acquired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, which it kept until the Ottoman conquest of 1570–1571.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>From the 15th century the history of the overseas empire was dominated by the Ottoman–Venetian wars, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> lost many territories and occasionally gained some, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> from the late 1680s to 1715. Its hold on Adriatic navigation was such that maps of the 17th and 18th centuries labelled the sea Mare di Venezia. After 1715 the remaining domains lay in Istria, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> and the Ionian Islands, and they were held until Napoleon I brought down the Republic in 1797. The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernand-braudel">Fernand Braudel</a> called islands such as Corfu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> and Cyprus Venice's motionless fleet.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stato da Màr” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Overseas, the <b>Stato da Mar</b> was ruled by appointed governors in Crete, Corfu, Dalmatia and, from 1489, Cyprus.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Republic took over the two islands, first through lessees and later through officials sent from Venice, making Tinos part of its overseas <b>Stato da Mar</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/venetian-tinos">Venetian Tinos</a></li></ul></section>
